message:
{{#is_warning}}
:warning: **istio-proxy sidecar is at {{value}}% of its memory limit.**
{{/is_warning}}
{{#is_alert}}
:rotating_light: **istio-proxy sidecar is at {{value}}% of its memory limit and is close to being OOMKilled.**
{{/is_alert}}

Pod: `{{kube_namespace.name}}/{{pod_name.name}}`
Cluster: `{{kube_cluster_name.name}}`

When the sidecar is OOMKilled the pod loses its mesh proxy, so all inbound and outbound traffic for that workload fails until the container restarts.

**Triage**
1. Check whether this is one pod or the whole mesh — the *Top 20 sidecars* panel on the [Istio — Memory & OOM Risk] dashboard answers that. Mesh-wide means a config problem, one pod means a workload problem.
2. Mesh-wide: check config size. Envoy holds the full mesh config in memory, so without a `Sidecar` resource limiting `egress.hosts`, sidecar memory grows with the number of services in the mesh. `istioctl proxy-config all <pod> -n <ns> | wc -l` shows how much config that proxy is carrying.
3. Single pod: check the *leak detector* panel for steady growth. A flat sawtooth is normal churn; a monotonic climb is a leak or an unbounded connection pool.
4. Short-term relief: raise `sidecar.istio.io/proxyMemoryLimit` on the pod annotation, or the global `global.proxy.resources.limits.memory` value.

{{#is_recovery}}:white_check_mark: Sidecar memory back under threshold.{{/is_recovery}}

